## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical incident engineering: how to fuse disparate signals, assign accountability, and make safe change decisions under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-signal correlation across metrics/logs/traces
- SLO-aligned alert routing and ownership mapping
- Canary and synthetic testing for regression prevention
- RCA templating and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rule mapping alerts to on-call owners
const owners = {
  payments: "team-payments",
  auth: "team-auth",
  inventory: "team-inventory"
};
function route(alert) {
  const svc = alert.tags?.service || "general";
  return owners[svc] || "oncall";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the end-to-end runbook in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to validate the success of the rollback strategy?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests SRE decision-making under pressure, understanding of error budget management, and the ability to balance business requirements with reliability commitments.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will: 1) Recognize that error budget exhaustion requires prioritizing reliability over new features, 2) Propose systematic performance optimization before introducing additional load, 3) Recommend a canary deployment strategy for controlled rollout, 4) Discuss comprehensive monitoring, alerting, and rollback procedures.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Error budget calculation and monitoring
const calculateErrorBudget = (slo: number, current: number) => {
  const errorBudget = (slo - current) / slo;
  return errorBudget > 0 ? errorBudget : 0;
};

// Feature rollout decision based on error budget
const canDeployFeature = (errorBudget: number, trafficIncrease: number) => {
  return errorBudget > (trafficIncrease * 0.1); // Conservative threshold
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to diagnose rate-limiter issues, design clear observability, and execute a controlled rollback with minimal disruption.

## Key Concepts
- Token bucket rate limiting across regions
- Observability for distributed systems
- Canary testing and safe traffic shifts
- Rollback and validation under time pressure
- Post-incident analysis and preventive fixes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token bucket core (simplified)
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) { this.rate = rate; this.capacity = capacity; this.tokens = capacity; this.last = Date.now(); }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) * this.rate / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + delta);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a beginner-friendly angle on data integrity under deploy pressure, focusing on per-tenant observability, controlled backpressure, and data replay safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant observability: lag, offsets, retry counts
- Ingress throttling and traffic-splitting
- Idempotent processing and dedup keys
- Rollback and data validation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple deduplicated idempotent processor
const seen = new Set();
function handle(event){
  const id = event.tenant + ":" + event.id;
  if (seen.has(id)) return;
  seen.add(id);
  // process event
}
```

